\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Gauge covariance and transport of selected laws}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For a [connection background](fdip-006D), the
\newvocab{gauge group} #{G^V} changes the frame independently at each
vertex. For #{g=(g_v)_{v\in V}}, define
##{\begin{aligned}
(gU)_e&=g_{h(e)}U_eg_{s(e)}^{-1},\\
(G_Vf)_v&=\rho(g_v)f_v,\\
(G_Ea)_e&=\rho(g_{h(e)})a_e.
\end{aligned}}
Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\extd_{gU}G_V&=G_E\extd_U,\\
Q_{gU}&=G_VQ_UG_V^{-1},\\
\det Q_{gU}&=\det Q_U.
\end{aligned}}
In particular, the massive spectra and Gaussian normalizers are gauge
invariant.}

\proof{
\p{On an edge with endpoints #{s,h},
##{\begin{aligned}
(\extd_{gU}G_Vf)(e)
&=\rho(g_h)f_h-\rho(g_hU_eg_s^{-1})\rho(g_s)f_s\\
&=\rho(g_h)(f_h-\rho(U_e)f_s).
\end{aligned}}
This proves the first identity. Both #{G_V} and #{G_E} are isometries,
and scalar weights imply #{G_E^*WG_E=W}. Taking adjoints in
#{\extd_{gU}=G_E\extd_UG_V^{-1}} therefore gives
##{\extd_{gU}^\dagger W\extd_{gU}=G_V\extd_U^\dagger W\extd_UG_V^{-1}.}
The mass term commutes with #{G_V}. Multiplicativity of the determinant
gives its invariance. The same isometry preserves real Euclidean volume,
or the real volume underlying a complex Hermitian space, so changing
variables also preserves the corresponding Gaussian integral.}
}

\p{For a fixed incidence presentation, #{U\mapsto gU} is a group
action: #{g_2(g_1U)=(g_2g_1)U}. Its action groupoid has connections
as objects and gauge transformations as arrows. If a selector is
included, use an orthogonal projector #{P} on real #{H}, or a
complex-linear orthogonal projector on complex #{H}, and transport it by
##{P'=G_VPG_V^{-1}.}
The identity, composition and inverse obey this equation by conjugation.
Together with relabelings that carry the connection values, these are
the connection counterparts of [selected-background arrows](fdip-005C).
The earlier scalar backgrounds and relabeling arrows are recovered with
#{F=\mathbb R}, #{G=\{1\}} and their original selector rank condition.}

\p{On the selected space #{X=\operatorname{im}P}, the compressed
inverse is #{C_P=(PQ_U^{-1}P)|_X}. The restriction
#{G_X:X\to\operatorname{im}P'} satisfies
##{C_{P'}=G_XC_PG_X^{-1}.}
Indeed insert the three conjugation identities for #{P'},
#{Q_{gU}^{-1}} and #{P'}. Inversion transports the selected precision.
For real fields this is exactly the
[selected Gaussian transport](fdip-005B); for complex fields the
Gaussian convention is specified in \ref{fdip-006I}.}

\p{Unitarity is essential for this argument. A nonorthogonal matrix
representation with an unchanged Euclidean metric need not satisfy
#{G_E^*WG_E=W}; changing connection matrices alone would then fail to
produce the asserted conjugacy.}
